cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "chatlens: text analysis of chat data from behavioural experiments"
message: "If you use this software in research, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Milella
    given-names: Nicola
repository-code: "https://github.com/nicomil/chatlens"
abstract: >-
  chatlens computes language measures, an LLM validation rubric and topic
  models over the conversations held during a behavioural experiment, and
  grafts them onto the choice datasets. An adapter turns one experiment's
  export into a canonical set of message tables; the analysis works from those
  tables alone, so the same pipeline runs on different studies.
keywords:
  - text analysis
  - experimental economics
  - content analysis
  - oTree
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0

references:
  - type: article
    title: "TopicGPT: A Prompt-based Topic Modeling Framework"
    authors:
      - family-names: Pham
        given-names: Chau Minh
      - family-names: Hoyle
        given-names: Alexander
      - family-names: Sun
        given-names: Simeng
      - family-names: Resnik
        given-names: Philip
      - family-names: Iyyer
        given-names: Mohit
    year: 2024
    notes: >-
      The topic stage drives the authors' own implementation; the prompts are
      part of the method and come from their repository.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      VADER: A Parsimonious Rule-based Model for Sentiment Analysis of Social
      Media Text
    authors:
      - family-names: Hutto
        given-names: Clayton
      - family-names: Gilbert
        given-names: Eric
    year: 2014
    notes: "The sentiment backend, used through the vaderSentiment package."
  - type: article
    title: "Crowdsourcing a Word-Emotion Association Lexicon"
    authors:
      - family-names: Mohammad
        given-names: Saif M.
      - family-names: Turney
        given-names: Peter D.
    journal: "Computational Intelligence"
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      The NRC Emotion Lexicon, behind the emotions finding. It is free for
      research and distributed through a request form, so chatlens does not
      ship it; anyone using that finding has obtained it under its own terms
      and should cite this.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Relatio: Text Semantics Capture Political and Economic Narratives
    authors:
      - family-names: Ash
        given-names: Elliott
      - family-names: Gauthier
        given-names: Germain
      - family-names: Widmer
        given-names: Philine
    journal: "Political Analysis"
    year: 2024
    notes: >-
      The relational analysis is their method and their package. chatlens
      prepares the input and reads the output; it does not reimplement the
      extraction, because an approximation of a published pipeline is not that
      pipeline.
